{=============================================================================
  Rigid 50 L vessel: heat removed from water at constant volume
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0230
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A closed rigid vessel of 50 L holds water at 120 C with a vapour quality
  of 0.6. The vessel is cooled at constant volume. The model computes the
  mass, the final quality and the heat transferred. Teaching exercise of
  ULiège MECA0002, thermodynamique appliquée, repetition 2 (2022-2023),
  not an exam. One vessel, initial and final states: no diagram-state arrays.

  Inputs : Vol, T_i, x_i, T_f
  Outputs: m_eau, x_f, Q
  Units  : SI - temperatures in K, pressures in Pa, energies in J
           (property calls receive °C)
  Source : ULiège MECA0002, R2_E1_2022.EES; the equation window names no
           author - see README.md
  Licence: MIT. Status: verified against the EES stored solution.
=============================================================================}

"!Problem"
"Exercise 1: a closed rigid vessel of 50 L contains water at 120 C with a quality of 60 percent, as in the original."
"The statement says the vessel is cooled to -10 C. The equation window stores +10 C. That stored temperature is kept."
"Calculate the heat transferred during the process, as in the original."

"!Data"
Vol = 50e-3 [m^3]                                        "vessel volume [m^3], 50 L as written"
T_i = 120 [K]+273.15 [K]                                 "initial temperature [K]; 120 C written as 120 K + 273.15 K, as stored"
x_i = 0.6                                                "initial vapour quality [-]"
T_f = 10 [K]+273.15 [K]                                  "final temperature [K]; the equation window stores 10 C, not the -10 C of the statement"

"!Resolution"
"The vessel is assumed closed and rigid. Its volume is constant, so no work is done, as in the original."
"The first law gives Q = U_2 - U_1 = m * (u_2 - u_1): the change of internal energy equals the heat transferred, as in the original."

"!Mass of water"
"The mass of water in the vessel is conserved because the vessel is closed, as in the original."
v_i = volume(Water, T=T_i-273.15, x=x_i)                "initial specific volume [m^3/kg]; temperature and quality are known, as in the original; the call takes °C and T_i is in K"
m_eau = Vol/v_i                                          "mass of water [kg], from the definition of specific volume"

"!Internal energy of the mixture"
u_i = intenergy(Water, t=t_i-273.15, x=x_i)             "initial specific internal energy [J/kg]; the original writes t_i; the call takes °C and returns J"

"!Final state"
"The specific volume is unchanged (volume and mass are conserved because the vessel is rigid and closed) and the temperature is known, as in the original."
v_f = v_i                                                "final specific volume [m^3/kg]"

x_f = quality(Water, T=T_f-273.15, v=v_f)               "final vapour quality [-]; the call takes °C and T_f is in K; v is the specific volume, as in the original"
u_f = intenergy(Water, T=T_f-273.15, x=x_f)             "final specific internal energy from temperature and quality [J/kg]; the call takes °C and returns J"
u_f_bis = intenergy(Water, T=T_f-273.15, v=v_f)         "final specific internal energy from temperature and specific volume [J/kg]; the call takes °C and returns J"

"!Heat transferred"
Q = m_eau*(u_f-u_i)                                      "heat transferred [J]; a negative Q means the system gives heat to the surroundings, as in the original"
